{"name":"White Moss","hex":"#d8d6c0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"moss","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various small, green, seedless plants growing on the ground or on the surfaces of trees, stones, etc.; now specifically, a plant of the phylum Bryophyta (formerly division Musci).","A kind or species of such plants."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with moss.","To cover (something) with moss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moss"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Moss","blocks":[{"text":"White Moss is an area lying on the common borders of the City of Manchester and the towns of Middleton and Chadderton in Greater Manchester."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Rusland_Court.jpg/330px-Rusland_Court.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Moss"},{"title":"Moss","blocks":[{"text":"Mosses are small, non-vascular flowerless plants in the taxonomic division Bryophyta sensu stricto. Bryophyta sensu lato may also refer to the parent group, bryophytes, which comprises liverworts, mosses, and hornworts. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple leaves that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a stem that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants. Mosses do not have seeds and after fertilisation develop sporophytes with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores. They are typically 0.2–10 cm (0.1–3.9 in) tall, though some species are much larger. Dawsonia superba, the tallest moss in the world, can grow to 60 cm (24 in) in height. There are approximately 12,000 species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg/330px-Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/White_Moss_Farm_off_Greenside_Way_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1158838.jpg/500px-White_Moss_Farm_off_Greenside_Way_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1158838.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/White_Moss_Farm_off_Greenside_Way_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1158838.jpg/500px-White_Moss_Farm_off_Greenside_Way_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1158838.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The former White Moss View Farm, now part of the Greengate Industrial Estate","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhite_Moss_Farm_off_Greenside_Way_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1158838.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg/500px-Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg/1280px-Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg/500px-Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chloroplasts (green discs) and accumulated starch granules in cells of Rosulabryum capillare","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg/500px-Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg/1280px-Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg/500px-Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Moss leaf under microscope, showing gemmae and a hair point (40×)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG/500px-Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG/1280px-Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG/500px-Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A patch of moss showing both gametophytes (the low, leaf-like forms) and sporophytes (the tall, stalk-like forms)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Haeckel_Muscinae.jpg/500px-Haeckel_Muscinae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Haeckel_Muscinae.jpg/1280px-Haeckel_Muscinae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Haeckel_Muscinae.jpg/500px-Haeckel_Muscinae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Muscinae\" from Ernst Haeckel's Kunstformen der Natur, 1904","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHaeckel_Muscinae.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/RedMoss.jpg/500px-RedMoss.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/RedMoss.jpg/500px-RedMoss.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bristly Haircap moss, a winter native of the Yorkshire Dales moorland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARedMoss.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Moss_on_a_tree%2C_on_a_hiking_trail_to_Twin_Falls%2C_near_Seattle.jpg/250px-Moss_on_a_tree%2C_on_a_hiking_trail_to_Twin_Falls%2C_near_Seattle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig